  "query_preview": "..(Wednesday night through Monday)\nIssued at 305 AM EST TUE NOV 10 2026\n\nThe extended period opens Wednesday night with a significant pattern\nshift. A northern-stream shortwave diving out of the Upper Midwest\ninto the Ohio Valley will carve out a deep, anomalous longwave\ntrough over the eastern third of the CONUS by Thursday. An attendant\ncold front will sweep across the Commonwealth late Wednesday night\ninto early Thursday morning, bringing increasing cloud cover and\nscattered to widespread rain.\n\nTemperatures Thursday will likely follow a non-diurnal curve. \nWith the cold front passing through during the morning hours, \ndaytime highs may be achieved around or shortly after daybreak, \nfollowed by falling or steady temperatures through the afternoon \nunder moderate cold air advection. While high-resolution guidance \nhas trended slightly warmer ahead of the boundary, opted to stick \nwith the NBM blend, yielding low 50s across the Bluegrass and \nupper 50s in the Big Sandy areas. As the trough deepens and \nstrengthens overhead, temperatures will continue to drop, with \nThursday night lows in the low to mid-30s.\n\nThe core of the upper trough will pivot just north of the region\nheading into Friday, pushing 500 mb heights below the 5th\nclimatological percentile and ushering in a brisk Canadian air mass\non breezy northwest winds. Highs Friday and Saturday will struggle\nto climb out of the 40s, with overnight lows in the mid to upper\n20s. Lingering post-frontal and upslope showers could mix with or\ntransition to the first flurries of the season across the higher\nterrain of southeast Kentucky Thursday night into Friday, and again\nFriday night.\n\nUpstream from this longwave trough, a high-amplitude ridge will\nbuild across the region, ushering in surface high pressure Sunday\nthrough Tuesday. Temperatures will gradually recover each day under\ndry weather and fair skies. Local 500 mb heights will rebound from\nbelow the 5th percentile on Friday to near the 80th percentile by\nTuesday evening. Temperatures will gradually warm from the low 50s\non Sunday to the upper 50s and low 60s by Tuesday, while nighttime\nlows remain at or slightly below freezing.",
